## Context
I'm building Chiefbot, an open-source agent app (github.com/JorgeMenaDev/chiefbot). A "chief of staff" agent hands work, such as a Jira ticket, to a "project" for one repo. The project starts "engineers". An engineer is an isolated agent that works on one ticket in its own Docker container and ends with a pull request. Two tickets can run at once, so two engineers run in parallel.

For our UI repo, an engineer cannot run or test the UI on its own. Each engineer needs its own full stack, isolated from every other engineer:
- the UI dev server
- the meta service (Java): all the API calls the UI makes
- the auth service (Java): authentication
- a PostgreSQL database, created fresh and seeded with test data

The setup order for each engineer is:
1. Start Postgres.
2. Run the migrations.
3. Seed the data.
4. Start auth.
5. Start meta.
6. Start the UI.
7. Wait until the health checks pass. Only then does the engineer start work.

